          "text": "     1→# CLAUDE.md\n     2→\n     3→This file provides guidance to Claude Code when working with this repository.\n     4→\n     5→## Project Overview\n     6→\n     7→`use-ai` is a TypeScript monorepo for building AI-powered React applications. It enables React components to expose tools (functions) to Claude AI via a Socket.IO server, allowing users to control UI through natural language.\n     8→\n     9→**Key Innovation**: Tools execute client-side where app state lives. Server coordinates between Claude API and client tools.\n    10→\n    11→**Monorepo Structure:**\n    12→- `packages/core/` - AG-UI protocol types and events\n    13→- `packages/client/` - React hooks (`useAI`)\n    14→- `packages/server/` - Socket.IO server with plugin support\n    15→- `packages/plugin-workflows/` - Headless workflow execution (server plugin)\n    16→- `packages/plugin-workflows-client/` - Client hooks for workflows (`useAIWorkflow`)\n    17→- `apps/example/` - Example todo app\n    18→\n    19→## Development Commands\n    20→\n    21→```bash\n    22→# Setup\n    23→bun install\n    24→export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...\n    25→\n    26→# Development (server must run first)\n    27→bun run start:server           # Port 8081\n    28→bun run dev                    # Port 3000\n    29→\n    30→# Building\n    31→bun run build                  # All packages\n    32→bun run build:client           # Client only\n    33→bun run build:server           # Server only\n    34→\n    35→# Testing\n    36→bun run test                   # Unit tests\n    37→bun run test:e2e               # E2E tests (requires ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)\n    38→bun run test:e2e:ui            # Interactive E2E UI\n    39→```\n    40→\n    41→### Environment Variables\n    42→\n    43→**Server:**\n    44→- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (required)\n    45→- `LOG_FORMAT` (optional): `pretty` (default) or `json`\n    46→- `RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS` (optional): Max requests per window (0 = unlimited)\n    47→- `RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS` (optional): Window in ms (default: 60000)\n    48→- `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY`, `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY`, `LANGFUSE_BASE_URL` (optional): LLM observability\n    49→\n    50→### WebSocket Protocol\n    51→\n    52→Use `wss://your-domain.com` for secure WebSocket connections. For local development without SSL, use `ws://localhost:8081`.\n    53→\n    54→## Core Architecture\n    55→\n    56→### Data Flow\n    57→\n    58→```\n    59→1. Client registers tools → Server stores definitions\n    60→2. User sends prompt + component state → Server forwards to Claude API\n    61→3. Claude returns tool_use blocks → Server sends tool_call to client\n    62→4. Client executes tool + waits for re-render → Sends tool_response back\n    63→5. Server sends results to Claude → Claude generates final response\n    64→```\n    65→\n    66→### Key Patterns\n    67→\n    68→#### Tool Definition\n    69→\n    70→```typescript\n    71→import { defineTool } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-client';\n    72→import { z } from 'zod';\n    73→\n    74→const addTodo = defineTool(\n    75→  'Add a new todo item to the list',\n    76→  z.object({ text: z.string() }),\n    77→  (input) => ({ success: true, message: 'Todo added' })\n    78→);\n    79→\n    80→// No arguments\n    81→const logout = defineTool('Log the user out', () => { /* ... */ });\n    82→\n    83→// Requires confirmation\n    84→const deleteAccount = defineTool(\n    85→  'Delete account permanently',\n    86→  () => { /* ... */ },\n    87→  { confirmationRequired: true }\n    88→);\n    89→```\n    90→\n    91→#### Component Integration\n    92→\n    93→```typescript\n    94→useAI({\n    95→  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n    96→  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`,  // State AI sees\n    97→  suggestions: ['Add a todo to buy groceries'],    // Empty chat suggestions (optional)\n    98→  invisible: true,                                  // For non-rendering components (optional)\n    99→  id: `Row ${rowIndex}`,                           // For multiple instances (optional)\n   100→});\n   101→```\n   102→\n   103→**State Management:**\n   104→- Component state provided via `prompt` argument (serialized to string)\n   105→- Library waits for re-render before sending tool response to get updated state\n   106→- Use `invisible: true` for components that don't re-render (e.g., providers)\n   107→- Use `id` to differentiate multiple instances (prefixes tool names: `Row 1/updateLabel`)\n   108→\n   109→**Suggestions:**\n   110→- Aggregated from all mounted hooks\n   111→- Up to 4 randomly selected suggestions shown in empty chat\n   112→- Click to send as message\n   113→\n   114→#### Socket.IO Protocol\n   115→\n   116→**Client → Server messages:**\n   117→- `run_agent`: User prompt with tools, state, conversation history (chat)\n   118→- `run_workflow`: Trigger headless workflow (use-ai extension)\n   119→- `tool_result`: Tool execution result\n   120→- `abort_run`: Cancel current run\n   121→\n   122→**Server → Client events:**\n   123→- `TEXT_MESSAGE_*`: Streaming text responses\n   124→- `TOOL_CALL_*`: Tool execution requests\n   125→- `RUN_*`: Lifecycle events (started, finished, error)\n   126→- `STATE_SNAPSHOT`: Current app state\n   127→- `MESSAGES_SNAPSHOT`: Conversation history\n   128→\n   129→### Plugin Architecture\n   130→\n   131→Server supports plugins for extensibility. All plugins implement `UseAIServerPlugin` interface.\n   132→\n   133→**Built-in Plugin:** `WorkflowsPlugin` for headless workflow execution.\n   134→\n   135→```typescript\n   136→import { UseAIServer, AISDKAgent } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-server';\n   137→import { WorkflowsPlugin, DifyWorkflowRunner } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-plugin-workflows';\n   138→import { anthropic } from '@ai-sdk/anthropic';\n   139→\n   140→const server = new UseAIServer({\n   141→  agents: { claude: new AISDKAgent({ model: anthropic('claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022') }) },\n   142→  defaultAgent: 'claude',\n   143→  plugins: [\n   144→    new WorkflowsPlugin({\n   145→      runners: new Map([\n   146→        ['dify', new DifyWorkflowRunner({\n   147→          apiBaseUrl: process.env.DIFY_API_URL || 'http://localhost:3001/v1',\n   148→          workflows: {\n   149→            'greeting-workflow': process.env.DIFY_GREETING_WORKFLOW_KEY!,\n   150→          },\n   151→        })],\n   152→      ]),\n   153→    }),\n   154→  ],\n   155→});\n   156→```\n   157→\n   158→### Agent vs WorkflowRunner\n   159→\n   160→**Agent** (`packages/server/src/agents/types.ts`):\n   161→- Handles conversational chat with history\n   162→- Used by `useAI` hook\n   163→- Default: `AISDKAgent` (uses Claude API via AI SDK)\n   164→\n   165→**WorkflowRunner** (`packages/plugin-workflows/src/types.ts`):\n   166→- Handles stateless, button-triggered operations\n   167→- Used by `useAIWorkflow` hook\n   168→- Example: `DifyWorkflowRunner` (integrates with Dify.AI)\n   169→\n   170→**Why separate?** Different lifecycles (multi-turn vs single-run), different integrations (LLMs vs workflow platforms).\n   171→\n   172→#### Using Workflows\n   173→\n   174→```typescript\n   175→import { useAIWorkflow } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-plugin-workflows-client';\n   176→\n   177→function PDFUploadButton() {\n   178→  const { trigger, status, text, error, connected } = useAIWorkflow('dify', 'greeting-workflow');\n   179→\n   180→  const handleClick = async () => {\n   181→    await trigger({\n   182→      inputs: { username: 'Alice' },\n   183→      tools: { /* tools workflow can call */ },\n   184→      onProgress: (progress) => console.log(progress),\n   185→      onComplete: (result) => console.log(result),\n   186→      onError: (err) => console.error(err),\n   187→    });\n   188→  };\n   189→\n   190→  return <button onClick={handleClick} disabled={!connected || status === 'running'}>Upload</button>;\n   191→}\n   192→```\n   193→\n   194→### Chat History\n   195→\n   196→Chat history is persisted to localStorage by default.\n   197→\n   198→**Features:**\n   199→- Auto-save messages\n   200→- Auto-generate titles from first user message\n   201→- Built-in chat management UI\n   202→- Storage limit: 20 most recent chats\n   203→\n   204→**Custom Storage:** Implement `ChatRepository` interface (see `packages/client/src/providers/chatRepository/types.ts`).\n   205→\n   206→**Programmatic Access:**\n   207→```typescript\n   208→const { currentChatId, createNewChat, loadChat, deleteChat, listChats, clearCurrentChat } = useAIContext();\n   209→```\n   210→\n   211→### Custom UI\n   212→\n   213→Replace default chat UI:\n   214→```typescript\n   215→<UseAIProvider serverUrl=\"wss://your-server.com\" CustomButton={MyButton} CustomChat={MyChat}>\n   216→  <App />\n   217→</UseAIProvider>\n   218→```\n   219→\n   220→## Observability\n   221→\n   222→Optional Langfuse integration tracks Claude API calls, token usage, tool calls, conversation history, costs, and errors. Enabled automatically when `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY` and `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` are set. See `packages/server/src/instrumentation.ts`.\n   223→\n   224→## Security\n   225→\n   226→- **Rate limiting**: Per-IP address (configured via env vars)\n   227→- **Client-only scope**: AI can only see/manipulate what's on the page\n   228→- **No backend API access**: V1 intentionally limits security risks\n   229→- **confirmationRequired**: Prompts AI for confirmation (not 100% foolproof)\n   230→\n   231→Following [Simon Willison's \"Lethal Trifecta\"](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/) mitigations.\n   232→\n   233→## Testing Strategy\n   234→\n   235→- **Unit tests**: Alongside source files, run with `bun test`\n   236→- **E2E tests**: Located in `apps/example/test/`, use Playwright with real Claude API calls\n   237→- **Langfuse integration tests**: Use Testcontainers (~3-4 min runtime), run with `bun run test:e2e:langfuse`\n   238→\n   239→E2E tests are preferred for AI features to catch edge cases with real API interactions.\n   240→\n   241→## Common Tasks\n   242→\n   243→### Adding a New Tool\n   244→1. Define tool with `defineTool()` in component\n   245→2. Add to `useAI` hook's `tools` object\n   246→3. Update component state in `prompt` argument\n   247→4. Write unit + E2E tests\n   248→\n   249→### Adding a New Feature\n   250→1. Update TypeScript types\n   251→2. Implement client-side changes first\n   252→3. Update server if needed (rare)\n   253→4. Add tests\n   254→5. Build packages: `bun run build:client` and/or `bun run build:server`\n   255→6. Verify in example app\n   256→\n   257→### Debugging Socket.IO Issues\n   258→1. Check server logs (`LOG_FORMAT=pretty`)\n   259→2. Check browser console\n   260→3. Verify WebSocket upgrade in DevTools Network tab\n   261→4. Use Socket.IO debug mode: `localStorage.debug = '*'`\n   262→\n   263→### Debugging AI Behavior\n   264→1. Check `prompt` being sent (component state)\n   265→2. Verify tool descriptions are clear\n   266→3. Check if multi-tool use is causing unexpected behavior\n   267→4. Review conversation history in server logs\n   268→5. Write E2E test to reproduce consistently\n   269→\n   270→## Implementation Details\n   271→\n   272→### Connection Management\n   273→- Socket.IO client per `useAI` hook\n   274→- Automatic reconnection with exponential backoff\n   275→- Provider monitors connection status\n   276→\n   277→### Error Handling\n   278→- Tool execution errors sent back to Claude as `tool_result`\n   279→- Zod validation errors propagate as tool execution errors\n   280→- Server errors sent as `error` messages to client\n   281→\n   282→### Build System\n   283→- Bun bundles code → TypeScript generates type declarations → ESM output\n   284→- Strict TypeScript mode enabled\n   285→- Never use `any` type unless unavoidable\n   286→\n   287→### File Naming\n   288→- React components: PascalCase (`UseAIFloatingButton.tsx`)\n   289→- Utilities/hooks: camelCase (`defineTool.ts`, `useAI.ts`)\n   290→\n   291→## Extensibility\n   292→\n   293→### Creating Custom Agents\n   294→\n   295→Implement `Agent` interface (see `packages/server/src/agents/types.ts`). Must emit AG-UI protocol events (`RUN_STARTED`, `TEXT_MESSAGE_*`, `TOOL_CALL_*`, `RUN_FINISHED`/`RUN_ERROR`).\n   296→\n   297→### Creating Custom WorkflowRunners\n   298→\n   299→Implement `WorkflowRunner` interface (see `packages/plugin-workflows/src/types.ts`). Must emit AG-UI protocol events.\n   300→\n   301→### Creating Custom Plugins\n   302→\n   303→Implement `UseAIServerPlugin` interface (see `packages/server/src/plugins/types.ts`). Register message handlers and optional lifecycle hooks.\n   304→\n   305→## Deployment\n   306→\n   307→Server designed for Kubernetes deployment:\n   308→- In-memory state only (Socket.IO sessions)\n   309→- Can be horizontally scaled (requires sticky sessions or Redis adapter)\n   310→- Shared across multiple applications\n   311→- Requires only `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable\n   312→\n   313→## Known Limitations (V1)\n   314→\n   315→- Conversation history is session-based (lost on disconnect)\n   316→- No authentication/authorization built-in\n   317→- Rate limiting is per-IP, not per-user\n   318→- No backend tool execution support\n   319→- Clustering requires sticky sessions or Redis adapter\n   320→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n",
