          "text": "     1→# @use-ai\n     2→\n     3→A React client/framework for easily enabling AI to control your users frontend.\n     4→\n     5→<img width=\"1137\" height=\"817\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e8dd5176-cca0-4104-9342-a6dea914f0f8\" />\n     6→\n     7→### [Demo video](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a0dd44e7-a64a-4106-afe2-49e5c8a1cbb4)\n     8→\n     9→## Table of Contents\n    10→\n    11→- [Overview](#overview)\n    12→- [Installation](#installation)\n    13→- [Quick Start](#quick-start)\n    14→- [Example](#example)\n    15→- [How it works](#how-it-works)\n    16→- [Why?](#why)\n    17→- [📦 Structure](#-structure)\n    18→- [Features](#features)\n    19→  - [General](#general)\n    20→    - [AG-UI Protocol](#ag-ui-protocol)\n    21→  - [Client](#client)\n    22→    - [`useAI` hook](#useai-hook)\n    23→    - [`UseAIProvider`](#useaiprovider)\n    24→    - [Component State via `prompt`](#component-state-via-prompt)\n    25→    - [Returning results of a tool to the AI](#returning-results-of-a-tool-to-the-ai)\n    26→    - [Tool Definition with Zod safety](#tool-definition-with-zod-safety)\n    27→    - [MultiTool Use](#multitool-use)\n    28→    - [Multiple Components of the same type](#multiple-components-of-the-same-type)\n    29→    - [Invisible (`Provider`) components](#invisible-provider-components)\n    30→    - [Suggestions](#suggestions)\n    31→    - [`confirmationRequired`](#confirmationrequired)\n    32→    - [Chat History](#chat-history)\n    33→    - [Error Code Mapping](#error-code-mapping)\n    34→    - [Using the AI directly (without chat UI)](#using-the-ai-directly-without-chat-ui)\n    35→    - [Custom UI](#custom-ui)\n    36→  - [Server](#server)\n    37→    - ['Batteries included' server](#batteries-included-server)\n    38→    - [`UseAIServer`](#useaiserver)\n    39→    - [External MCPs](#external-mcps)\n    40→    - [Rate Limiting](#rate-limiting)\n    41→    - [Langfuse](#langfuse)\n    42→  - [Plugins](#plugins)\n    43→    - [`@meetsmore/use-ai-plugin-workflows`](#use-aiplugin-workflows)\n    44→\n    45→## Overview\n    46→\n    47→**TodoList.tsx**\n    48→```typescript\n    49→export default function TodoList() {\n    50→  const { todos, addTodo, deleteTodo, toggleTodo } = useTodoLogic();\n    51→\n    52→  const { ref } = useAI({\n    53→    tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo, toggleTodo },\n    54→    prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`,\n    55→  });\n    56→}\n    57→```\n    58→\n    59→**index.tsx**\n    60→```typescript\n    61→root.render(\n    62→  <UseAIProvider serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8081\">\n    63→    <App />\n    64→  </UseAIProvider>\n    65→);\n    66→```\n    67→\n    68→1. Components call `useAI` to declare their tools and state (`prompt`) to `use-ai`.\n    69→2. `UseAIProvider` provides a floating-action-button chat UI and aggregates `useAI` tools + prompts from all child components.\n    70→3. `@meetsmore/use-ai-server` acts as a co-ordinator between your frontend and an LLM.\n    71→4. ✨ The LLM can now call your `tools` functions in the frontend as MCPs.\n    72→\n    73→## Installation\n    74→\n    75→**Frontend**\n    76→```bash\n    77→bun add @meetsmore/use-ai-client\n    78→```\n    79→\n    80→## Quick Start\n    81→\n    82→Define your component, and call `useAI` with some tools.\n    83→\n    84→```typescript\n    85→function MyTextbox() {\n    86→  const [text, setText] = useState()\n    87→\n    88→  useAI({\n    89→    tools: {\n    90→      setText: defineTool(\n    91→        'Set the text of the textbox.',             // description of the tool\n    92→        z.object({                                  // zod schema for declaring inputs\n    93→          text: z.string().describe('The new text')\n    94→        }),\n    95→        (input) => {                                // the tool callback to run\n    96→          setText(input)                            // <-- your function\n    97→          return { success: true }                  // result to send back to the LLM\n    98→        }\n    99→      )\n   100→    },\n   101→    prompt: `The textbox text is ${text}`           // The state of the component in text form, for the LLM. \n   102→  })\n   103→\n   104→  return (\n   105→    <p>{{text}}</p>\n   106→  )\n   107→}\n   108→\n   109→export default function App() {\n   110→  return (\n   111→    <UseAIProvider serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8081\">\n   112→      <MyTextbox/>\n   113→    </UseAIProvider>\n   114→  )\n   115→}\n   116→```\n   117→\n   118→Run the `@meetsmore/use-ai-server`:\n   119→\n   120→```bash\n   121→> TODO: Add docker details here\n   122→```\n   123→\n   124→Start your frontend:\n   125→\n   126→```bash\n   127→bun dev\n   128→```\n   129→\n   130→## Example\n   131→\n   132→If you just want to play with a working example:\n   133→\n   134→```bash\n   135→export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY='xxxxx-your-anthropic-api-key-here-xxxxxx'\n   136→git clone git@github.com:meetsmore/use-ai.git\n   137→bun install\n   138→bun dev\n   139→```\n   140→\n   141→Visit http://localhost:3000 to see some examples of `use-ai` in action.\n   142→The example app code is in `apps/example`.\n   143→\n   144→## How it works\n   145→\n   146→![architecture diagram](assets/architecture.png)\n   147→\n   148→1. [client] `useAI` calls provide javascript functions with metadata to be used as tools.\n   149→2. [client] `UseAIProvider` collects all mounted components with `useAI` hooks and sends their tools to a `UseAIServer`.\n   150→3. [server] `UseAIServer` co-ordinates between the clientside and the LLM, providing the clientside tools as MCP tools to the LLM.\n   151→4. [LLM] The LLM agent runs and invokes clientside tools if needed.\n   152→5. [server] The server requests the clientside invoke the clientside tool with the desired arguments from the LLM.\n   153→6. [client] The client invokes the requested function with its arguments.\n   154→\n   155→## Why?\n   156→\n   157→You can get a large amount of power from `use-ai`, even by only implementing a handful of tools.\n   158→This is partly because `use-ai` supports **MultiTool calls**, so the LLM can ask to batch execute tools in one generation step, which the frontend can then do all at once.\n   159→\n   160→For example, with our todo list example:\n   161→\n   162→```typescript\n   163→export default function TodoList() {\n   164→  const { todos, addTodo, deleteTodo, toggleTodo } = useTodoLogic();\n   165→\n   166→  const { ref } = useAI({\n   167→    tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo, toggleTodo },\n   168→    prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`,\n   169→  });\n   170→}\n   171→```\n   172→\n   173→We can already achieve the following in one shot:\n   174→\n   175→- 'Add a shopping list to bake a new york cheesecake'.\n   176→- 'I already have all the sweet ingredients, check them off.'\n   177→\n   178→Even with only **add**, **delete**, and **toggle**, you can already unlock quite a lot of power.\n   179→\n   180→Because the tools are all clientside, we don't need to worry about auth for the MCP tools, because we are only doing things that the clientside application can already do (as we're invoking clientside code).\n   181→\n   182→## 📦 Structure\n   183→\n   184→```bash\n   185→├── apps\n   186→│   └── example             # example app\n   187→├── packages\n   188→│   ├── client              # frontend React library\n   189→│   ├── core                # shared types\n   190→│   └── server              # backend server library / server instance\n   191→```\n   192→\n   193→# Features\n   194→\n   195→## General\n   196→\n   197→### AG-UI Protocol\n   198→\n   199→`@use-ai` partially implements the [AG-UI protocol](https://docs.ag-ui.com/introduction) for communication between `@meetsmore/use-ai-client` and `@meetsmore/use-ai-server`.\n   200→\n   201→Not all aspects of AG-UI protocol are implemented now, but it feel free to open a PR to add any parts of the protocol you need.\n   202→\n   203→There are some minor extensions to the protocol:\n   204→\n   205→**Message Types**:\n   206→- `run_workflow`: Trigger headless workflow (use-ai extension) [see `@meetsmore/use-ai-plugin-workflows`]\n   207→\n   208→## Client\n   209→\n   210→### `useAI` hook\n   211→\n   212→The fundamental building block for adding AI capabilities to any React component:\n   213→\n   214→```tsx\n   215→import { useAI, defineTool } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-client';\n   216→import { z } from 'zod';\n   217→\n   218→function TodoList() {\n   219→  const [todos, setTodos] = useState<Todo[]>([]);\n   220→\n   221→  // Define a tool the AI can call\n   222→  const addTodo = defineTool(\n   223→    'Add a new todo item to the list',\n   224→    z.object({\n   225→      text: z.string().describe('The text content of the todo item'),\n   226→    }),\n   227→    (input) => {\n   228→      const newTodo = {\n   229→        id: Date.now(),\n   230→        text: input.text.trim(),\n   231→        completed: false,\n   232→      };\n   233→      setTodos(prev => [...prev, newTodo]);\n   234→      return { success: true, message: `Added todo: \"${input.text}\"` };\n   235→    }\n   236→  );\n   237→\n   238→  // Register tools and provide current state to AI\n   239→  useAI({\n   240→    tools: { addTodo },\n   241→    prompt: `Current todos: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n   242→  });\n   243→\n   244→  return (/* your UI */);\n   245→}\n   246→```\n   247→\n   248→### `UseAIProvider`\n   249→\n   250→```tsx\n   251→import { UseAIProvider } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-client';\n   252→\n   253→root.render(\n   254→  <UseAIProvider\n   255→    serverUrl=\"wss://your-server.com\"\n   256→    systemPrompt=\"Be concise and friendly in your responses.\" // General system prompt, if you want to change it.\n   257→  >\n   258→    <App />\n   259→  </UseAIProvider>\n   260→);\n   261→```\n   262→\n   263→### Component State via `prompt`\n   264→\n   265→When you call `useAI`, you can provide a prompt that is used to tell the LLM the state of the component in a text-friendly way.\n   266→\n   267→```tsx\n   268→  useAI({\n   269→    // tools are optional, maybe you only want to expose state to the AI!\n   270→    prompt: `Current todos: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n   271→  });\n   272→```\n   273→\n   274→If `tools` or `prompt` change, they will cause `useAI` to be re-rendered, so the LLM will always have the latest state whenever you invoke it.\n   275→\n   276→### Returning results of a tool to the AI\n   277→\n   278→While `prompt` is good enough to reflect state of a component, your tool call may not update state, or you may trigger side effects.\n   279→\n   280→`useAI` tools can return a result back to the AI:\n   281→\n   282→```tsx\n   283→useAI({\n   284→  tools: {\n   285→    sendEmail: defineTool(\n   286→      `Send an email on behalf of the user.`,\n   287→      z.object({\n   288→        to: z.string().describe('The address to send the email to.')\n   289→        body: z.string().describe('The email content.')\n   290→      }),\n   291→      (input) => {\n   292→        sendEmail(input.to, input.body);\n   293→        return {\n   294→          success: true,\n   295→          message: `Email was sent to ${input.to}.`,\n   296→          body: input.body\n   297→        };\n   298→      }\n   299→    ),\n   300→  }\n   301→})\n   302→```\n   303→\n   304→### Tool Definition with Zod safety\n   305→\n   306→When you use `defineTool`, zod schemas are used to define the input arguments for the tool.\n   307→These are used for validation (to ensure the LLM didn't generate nonsense for your arguments).\n   308→The types of the callback function are also matched against the types of the zod schema, so you will get TypeScript errors if they don't match.\n   309→\n   310→### MultiTool Use\n   311→\n   312→LLMs can invoke multiple tools at once (return multiple tool calls in a response).\n   313→These are handled in order by `useAI`, but in one batch, which means that you can get bulk-editing functionality just by declaring single-item mutations.\n   314→\n   315→```tsx\n   316→useAI({\n   317→  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n   318→  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n   319→});\n   320→```\n   321→\n   322→User: *\"add a shopping list to make tonkotsu ramen\"*\n   323→\n   324→The AI automatically calls `addTodo` multiple times for each ingredient, even though you only defined single-item operations.\n   325→\n   326→### Multiple Components of the same type\n   327→\n   328→Use the `id` parameter to differentiate between component instances.\n   329→\n   330→You should use something that the AI can contextually understand, rather than a randomly generated UUID.\n   331→\n   332→```tsx\n   333→function ListItem({ rowIndex, label, counter, color }) {\n   334→  useAI({\n   335→    tools: { updateLabel, incrementCounter, changeColor },\n   336→    prompt: `Current state - Label: \"${label}\", Counter: ${counter}, Color: \"${color}\"`,\n   337→    id: `Row ${rowIndex}`  // AI uses this to target specific rows\n   338→  });\n   339→}\n   340→```\n   341→\n   342→Or use the component's `id` attribute:\n   343→\n   344→```tsx\n   345→<ListItem id=\"Row 1\" />  // Automatically used as useAI id\n   346→```\n   347→\n   348→### Invisible (`Provider`) components\n   349→\n   350→You may want to expose AI tools from structural components rather than visual ones.\n   351→A common use case for this is to provide 'global' tools that are always accessible to the AI on every page, and not bound to a specific component.\n   352→\n   353→You need to tell `useAI` that the component will not re-render when a tool call happens, by providing the `invisible: true` argument.\n   354→\n   355→```tsx\n   356→function MyAppRouter() {\n   357→  useAI({\n   358→    tools: { navigateTo },\n   359→    invisible: true  // Don't wait for re-render after tool calls\n   360→  });\n   361→}\n   362→```\n   363→\n   364→### Suggestions\n   365→\n   366→If the user opens a brand new chat, it's helpful to give them a call-to-action prompt that they can use, to understand what they can do with your app using AI.\n   367→\n   368→You can do this using the `suggestions` argument of `useAI`:\n   369→\n   370→\n   371→```tsx\n   372→function MyAppRouter() {\n   373→  useAI({\n   374→    tools: { navigateTo },\n   375→    invisible: true  // Don't wait for re-render after tool calls\n   376→    suggestions: [\n   377→      'Go to my profile page.',\n   378→      'Show me the jobs page.'\n   379→    ]\n   380→  });\n   381→}\n   382→```\n   383→\n   384→The `UseAIProvider` chat selects 4 random suggestions from all mounted components for display in empty chat pages, users can click them to instantly send them as a message.\n   385→\n   386→### `confirmationRequired`\n   387→\n   388→For destructive operations, use `confirmationRequired`:\n   389→\n   390→```tsx\n   391→const deleteAccount = defineTool(\n   392→  'Delete this user\\'s account permanently',\n   393→  () => { /* deletion logic */ },\n   394→  { confirmationRequired: true }\n   395→);\n   396→```\n   397→\n   398→This will try its best to get the AI to request confirmation from the user via chat before taking action.\n   399→\n   400→### Chat History\n   401→\n   402→By default, there is locally stored chat history for up to 20 chats.\n   403→\n   404→The user can switch between them and resume old chats.\n   405→\n   406→If you wanted to have chats stored on the server, with the users account, you can provide your own `ChatRepository` implementation to do that:\n   407→\n   408→```tsx\n   409→import { UseAIProvider } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-client';\n   410→\n   411→root.render(\n   412→  <UseAIProvider\n   413→    serverUrl=\"wss://your-server.com\"\n   414→    chatRepository={{new MyChatRepository()}} // define your own chat repository for storing history.\n   415→  >\n   416→    <App />\n   417→  </UseAIProvider>\n   418→);\n   419→```\n   420→\n   421→### Error Code Mapping\n   422→\n   423→There are errors which can occur when using LLM APIs, (e.g. rate limiting, overload, etc).\n   424→These are defined internally using error codes:\n   425→\n   426→```typescript\n   427→/**\n   428→ * Error codes sent from server to client.\n   429→ * Used to identify specific error types for proper handling and messaging.\n   430→ */\n   431→export enum ErrorCode {\n   432→  /** Error when AI API is experiencing high load (HTTP 529) */\n   433→  API_OVERLOADED = 'API_OVERLOADED',\n   434→  /** Error when rate limit is exceeded (HTTP 429) */\n   435→  RATE_LIMITED = 'RATE_LIMITED',\n   436→  /** Generic error for unknown or unexpected errors */\n   437→  UNKNOWN_ERROR = 'UNKNOWN_ERROR',\n   438→}\n   439→```\n   440→\n   441→On the client, you will want to show friendly errors to the user.\n   442→By default, there are reasonable messages in English, but if you needed to localize them to another language, you can pass your own mapping of error codes -> strings:\n   443→\n   444→```tsx\n   445→import { UseAIProvider } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-client';\n   446→\n   447→root.render(\n   448→  <UseAIProvider\n   449→    serverUrl=\"wss://your-server.com\"\n   450→    errorMessages={{\n   451→      API_OVERLOADED: \"Le service IA est actuellement surchargé.\",\n   452→      RATE_LIMITED: \"Trop de requêtes.\",\n   453→      UNKNOWN_ERROR: \"Une erreur s'est produite.\"\n   454→    }}\n   455→  >\n   456→    <App />\n   457→  </UseAIProvider>\n   458→);\n   459→```\n   460→\n   461→### Using the AI directly (without chat UI)\n   462→\n   463→> TODO: This needs to be easier, using the client currently is awkward.\n   464→>       User should get a similar interface to `useAIWorkflow`. \n   465→\n   466→```typescript\n   467→const { \n   468→  serverUrl,\n   469→  connected,\n   470→  registerTools,\n   471→  unregisterTools,\n   472→  updatePrompt,\n   473→  client,\n   474→  currentChatId,\n   475→  createNewChat, \n   476→  loadChat, \n   477→  deleteChat, \n   478→  listChats, \n   479→  clearCurrentChat \n   480→} = useAIContext();\n   481→```\n   482→\n   483→### Custom UI\n   484→\n   485→If you don't like the default UI, you can customize both the floating-action-button and the chat UI itself.\n   486→\n   487→```tsx\n   488→import { UseAIProvider } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-client';\n   489→\n   490→root.render(\n   491→  <UseAIProvider\n   492→    serverUrl=\"wss://your-server.com\"\n   493→    CustomButton={MyCustomButton}\n   494→    CustomChat={MyCustomChat}\n   495→  >\n   496→    <App />\n   497→  </UseAIProvider>\n   498→);\n   499→```\n   500→\n   501→You can also disable them by passing `null`:\n   502→\n   503→```tsx\n   504→import { UseAIProvider } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-client';\n   505→\n   506→root.render(\n   507→  <UseAIProvider\n   508→    serverUrl=\"wss://your-server.com\"\n   509→    CustomButton={null}\n   510→    CustomChat={null}\n   511→  >\n   512→    <App />\n   513→  </UseAIProvider>\n   514→);\n   515→```\n   516→\n   517→## Server\n   518→\n   519→### 'Batteries included' server\n   520→\n   521→For most use cases, you can just use `@meetsmore/use-ai-server` as-is, and customize only the environment variables:\n   522→\n   523→```bash\n   524→# AI Provider (at least one required)\n   525→ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n   526→# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-20250514\n   527→# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n   528→# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4-turbo\n   529→\n   530→# Dify Workflow Runner (optional)\n   531→# DIFY_API_URL=http://localhost:3001/v1\n   532→\n   533→# Remote MCP Server Configuration\n   534→# MCP_ENDPOINT_YOURMCPNAME_URL=http://localhost:3002\n   535→# MCP_ENDPOINT_YOURMCPNAME_NAMESPACE=yourmcpname  # Optional, defaults to \"yourmcpname\"\n   536→# MCP_ENDPOINT_YOURMCPNAME_TIMEOUT=60000          # Optional, defaults to 30000\n   537→\n   538→# Server Configuration (optional)\n   539→# PORT=8081\n   540→# LOG_FORMAT=pretty\n   541→\n   542→# Rate Limiting (optional)\n   543→# RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=0\n   544→# RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000\n   545→\n   546→# Langfuse Observability (optional)\n   547→# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n   548→# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n   549→# LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=https://cloud.langfuse.com\n   550→```\n   551→\n   552→### `UseAIServer`\n   553→\n   554→If you want to integrate the `use-ai` server into your existing server, for example if you don't want to deploy another instance in your infrastructure, or you want to use some capabilities in your existing server, you can use `@meetsmore/use-ai-server` as a library and run an instance of `UseAIServer`:\n   555→\n   556→```typescript\n   557→  const server = new UseAIServer({\n   558→    port: 8081,\n   559→    agents: {\n   560→      'claude': new AISDKAgent({ /** see AISDKAgent for an example */ })\n   561→    },\n   562→    defaultAgent: 'claude',\n   563→    rateLimitMaxRequests: 1_000,\n   564→    rateLimitWindowMs: 60_000,\n   565→    plugins: [              // see 'Plugins'\n   566→      new WorkflowsPlugin({ /** see @meetsmore/use-ai-plugin-workflows */})\n   567→    ],\n   568→    mcpEndpoints: [{        // see 'External MCPs'\n   569→      url: 'http://my-app.com/mcp',\n   570→      namespace: 'my-app',\n   571→      timeout: 30_000\n   572→    }],\n   573→  });\n   574→```\n   575→\n   576→### External MCPs\n   577→\n   578→`use-ai` supports providing additional tools using external MCPs, defined by `mcpEndpoints`.\n   579→These MCP endpoints should follow the MCP protocol to return a set of tools when called.\n   580→\n   581→The server will invoke these on start, with a refresh interval to reload them periodically.\n   582→\n   583→To configure these in `@meetsmore/use-ai-server`, you can use the environment variables:\n   584→\n   585→```bash\n   586→# MCP_ENDPOINT_YOURMCPNAME_URL=http://localhost:3002\n   587→# MCP_ENDPOINT_YOURMCPNAME_NAMESPACE=yourmcpname  # Optional, defaults to \"yourmcpname\"\n   588→# MCP_ENDPOINT_YOURMCPNAME_TIMEOUT=60000          # Optional, defaults to 30000\n   589→\n   590→# multiple endpoints are supported\n   591→# MCP_ENDPOINT_ANOTHERMCP_URL=http://localhost:3003\n   592→```\n   593→\n   594→If your MCP tools need auth (e.g. you want to do things on behalf of the user, in the backend), you can use the `@meetsmore/use-ai-client` `mcpHeadersProvider` prop to do that:\n   595→\n   596→```tsx\n   597→import { UseAIProvider } from '@meetsmore/use-ai-client';\n   598→\n   599→root.render(\n   600→  <UseAIProvider\n   601→    serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8081\"\n   602→    mcpHeadersProvider={() => ({\n   603→      'http://localhost:3002/*': {        // when any URL matching this pattern is called by the server for MCPs....\n   604→        headers: { 'X-API-Key': 'secret-api-key-123' },   // add these headers to the request.\n   605→      },\n   606→    })}\n   607→  >\n   608→    <App/>\n   609→  </UseAIProvider>\n   610→);\n   611→```\n   612→\n   613→[picomatch](https://github.com/micromatch/picomatch) is used for patterns, so you can use any `picomatch` compatible pattern.\n   614→\n   615→The flow works like this:\n   616→\n   617→1. [client] The user sends a message to the AI.\n   618→2. [client] `use-ai` calls the `mcpHeadersProvider` and passes the full 'header map' to the server.\n   619→3. [server] The server prompts the LLM.\n   620→4. [LLM] The LLM decides to call a tool.\n   621→5. [server] The server checks if the call will use a remote MCP, if it will, it adds the headers matching the URL pattern.\n   622→\n   623→### Rate Limiting\n   624→\n   625→`UseAIServer` supports rate limiting by IP.\n   626→This allows you to implement `use-ai` without auth, and just rely on rate limiting to prevent abuse of your token spend.\n   627→\n   628→You can configure it using environment variables if using `@meetsmore/use-ai-server` directly:\n   629→\n   630→``` bash\n   631→# Rate Limiting (optional)\n   632→RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=0\n   633→RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000\n   634→```\n   635→\n   636→Or you can use arguments to `UseAIServer`:\n   637→\n   638→```typescript\n   639→  const server = new UseAIServer({\n   640→    port: 8081,\n   641→    agents: {\n   642→      'claude': new AISDKAgent({ /** see AISDKAgent for an example */ })\n   643→    },\n   644→    defaultAgent: 'claude',\n   645→    rateLimitMaxRequests: 1_000,\n   646→    rateLimitWindowMs: 60_000,\n   647→  });\n   648→```\n   649→\n   650→### Langfuse\n   651→\n   652→[Langfuse](https://langfuse.com/) is an AI observability platform that provides insights into your AI usage.\n   653→The `use-ai` `AISDKAgent` supports this out of the box, just set these environment variables:\n   654→\n   655→```bash\n   656→LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY='your-langfuse-public-key'\n   657→LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY='your-langfuse-secret-key'\n   658→```\n   659→\n   660→## Plugins\n   661→\n   662→`@meetsmore/use-ai-server` has a plugin architecture allowing you to extend the AG-UI protocol and add more handlers.\n   663→\n   664→```typescript\n   665→export interface UseAIServerPlugin {\n   666→  /**\n   667→   * Returns the unique identifier for this plugin.\n   668→   * Used for logging and debugging purposes.\n   669→   *\n   670→   * @returns Plugin name (e.g., 'workflows', 'analytics', 'auth')\n   671→   */\n   672→  getName(): string;\n   673→\n   674→  /**\n   675→   * Called when the plugin is registered with the server.\n   676→   * Use this to register custom message handlers.\n   677→   *\n   678→   * @param server - Object with registerMessageHandler method\n   679→   */\n   680→  registerHandlers(server: {\n   681→    registerMessageHandler(type: string, handler: MessageHandler): void;\n   682→  }): void;\n   683→\n   684→  /**\n   685→   * Optional lifecycle hook called when a client connects.\n   686→   *\n   687→   * @param session - The newly created client session\n   688→   */\n   689→  onClientConnect?(session: ClientSession): void;\n   690→\n   691→  /**\n   692→   * Optional lifecycle hook called when a client disconnects.\n   693→   *\n   694→   * @param session - The disconnecting client session\n   695→   */\n   696→  onClientDisconnect?(session: ClientSession): void;\n   697→}\n   698→```\n   699→\n   700→This is primarily used to avoid polluting the main library with the code used for providing workflow runners (see `@meetsmore/use-ai-plugin-workflows`)\n   701→\n   702→### `@meetsmore/use-ai-plugin-workflows`\n   703→\n   704→`@meetsmore/use-ai-plugin-workflows` provides the capability for running workflows using AI workflow engines like Dify.\n   705→\n   706→Only `DifyWorkflowRunner` is supported for now, but you can write your own Runners very easily (feel free to open a PR).\n   707→\n   708→```typescript\n   709→  const server = new UseAIServer({\n   710→    port: 8081,\n   711→    agents: {\n   712→      'claude': new AISDKAgent({ /** see AISDKAgent for an example */ })\n   713→    },\n   714→    defaultAgent: 'claude',\n   715→    plugins: [\n   716→      new WorkflowsPlugin({ runners: {\n   717→        'dify': new DifyWorkflowRunner({\n   718→          apiBaseUrl: process.env.DIFY_API_URL,\n   719→          workflows: {\n   720→            'greeting-workflow': 'get-this-value-from-dify'\n   721→          }\n   722→        })\n   723→      }})\n   724→    ]\n   725→  });\n   726→```\n   727→\n   728→```tsx\n   729→  // define an existing `dify` workflow.\n   730→  const { trigger, status, text, error, connected } = useAIWorkflow('dify', 'get-this-value-from-dify');\n   731→\n   732→  // Trigger the workflow.\n   733→  await trigger({\n   734→    inputs: {\n   735→      username: 'Alice',\n   736→    },\n   737→    tools: {\n   738→      displayGreeting: defineTool(\n   739→        'Display a greeting message to the user',\n   740→        z.object({\n   741→          greeting: z.string().describe('The greeting message to display'),\n   742→        }),\n   743→        (input) => {\n   744→          addLog(`Tool called: displayGreeting`);\n   745→          setProcessedItems((prev) => [...prev, input.greeting]);\n   746→          return { success: true };\n   747→        }\n   748→      ),\n   749→    },\n   750→    onProgress: (progress) => {\n   751→      addLog(`Progress: ${progress.status}${progress.text ? ` - ${progress.text}` : ''}`);\n   752→    },\n   753→    onComplete: (result) => {\n   754→      addLog('Workflow completed!');\n   755→    },\n   756→    onError: (err) => {\n   757→      addLog(`Error: ${err.message}`);\n   758→    },\n   759→  });\n   760→```\n   761→\n   762→Because it's awkward to get API keys for workflows from Dify, you can use a mapping of names -> API keys:\n   763→\n   764→```typescript\n   765→    plugins: [\n   766→      new WorkflowsPlugin({ runners: {\n   767→        'dify': new DifyWorkflowRunner({\n   768→          apiBaseUrl: process.env.DIFY_API_URL,\n   769→          workflows: {\n   770→            'greeting-workflow': 'x7a$978s998290abhdg' // memorable name -> dify API key value\n   771→          }\n   772→        })\n   773→      }})\n   774→    ]\n   775→```\n   776→\n   777→```tsx\n   778→  const { trigger, status, text, error, connected } = useAIWorkflow('dify', 'greeting-workflow');\n   779→```\n   780→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n",
